{"question":"What does 'that' (dhālika) refer to, and why is it masculine?","words":["ذَٰلِكَ"],"type":"grammar","positions":[{"position":"It refers to the seven years; the masculine demonstrative is used because the number sabʿ has no feminine marker and resembles a masculine form.","reasons":"Al-Kalbī (Wāḥidī, Ibn al-Jawzī): sabʿ has no feminine marker in its form; Ibn al-Qāsim (Ibn al-Jawzī): the number resembles a masculine form; al-Kalbī supports it with a verse where masculine grammar follows a feminine noun lacking a feminine marker.","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r3","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r1","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It refers to the drought, not to the years.","reasons":"Muqātil (reported by Wāḥidī and Ibn al-Jawzī).","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r4","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether the masculine demonstrative can point back to the feminine years (because sabʿ lacks a feminine marker) or points instead to the drought."}
{"question":"Why is the coming year called ʿām?","words":["عَامٌۭ"],"type":"rhetoric","positions":[{"position":"ʿĀm often suggests ease and fertility, whereas sana often suggests hardship and famine; the positive term signals the change from the preceding years.","reasons":"Ālūsī: both mean year, but differ in connotation.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is a full twelve months, and calling it ʿām signals that its coming relief is known and controlled by God alone.","reasons":"Biqāʿī.","rows":["BIQAI-FULL:v4p52/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"Why is 'in it' (fīhi) repeated and fronted?","words":["فِيهِ"],"type":"rhetoric","positions":[{"position":"The repetition distinguishes rain and relief, which are acts of God, from pressing, which is an act people perform.","reasons":"Bursevi.","rows":["BURSEVI:v4p174/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The repetition signals different times or kinds of benefit, enumerates the year's blessings, or stresses that the drought's rain was negligible beside this year's; fronting marks the focus.","reasons":"Ālūsī; he adds that rhyme is also possible as a reason for the second occurrence.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What does yughāthu al-nās mean: that people receive rain or that they are relieved?","words":["يُغَاثُ ٱلنَّاسُ"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"People receive rain (ghayth).","reasons":"Ibn ʿAbbās, Mujāhid, Qatāda, al-Ḍaḥḥāk and most exegetes (Ṭabarī, Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Māwardī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ibn al-Jawzī, Abū Ḥayyān, Ālūsī, Durr); Muqātil, ʿAbd al-Razzāq (Qatāda), Wāḥidī (Wajīz), Ibn Kathīr: a year of rain; ghayth itself means rain (Ibn Qutayba, Thaʿlabī); Ibn al-Sikkīt: rain falling on the land (Wāḥidī, Rāzī); derived from triliteral ghāth (Abū Ḥayyān; Samīn: ghīthat al-bilād, ghāthanā Allāh, and a Bedouin woman's 'ghithnā mā shiʾnā'; Ālūsī; Bursevi, who explains the alif from the root vowel; Biqāʿī: ghayth has three- and four-letter forms); Qurṭubī: rain, whether derived from relief or ghawth, distinguishing the related forms; Ṭabrisī: ghayth is rain arriving when needed; Ṭabāṭabāʾī prefers it because the following pressing fits it, and rejects the objection that Egypt depends on the Nile since its flood is supplied by rain in Sudan (Asad likewise: the Nile floods are fed by upstream rainfall); al-Ṣādiq takes it from rain rather than rescue (Ṭabāṭabāʾī, via al-ʿAyyāshī and ʿAlī b. Muʿammar), and also says the revealed wording of the preceding verse is 'what you bring near for them'; Iṣlāḥī reports some take it as rain and says God sends abundant rain.","rows":["MUQATIL:12:48-51/r2","ABDURRAZZAQ:v2p324#3/r3","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p218#5/r1","TAB-FULL:v13p194/r3","TAB-FULL:v13p194/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r1","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r2","THALABI:v5p227/r7","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r27","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r5","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:12:47-50/r5","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r3","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r2","TABRISI:12:43-49#2/r11","TABRISI:12:47-49/r10","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r5","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r4","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r2","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v4p393/r2","BIQAI-FULL:v4p52/r5","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r3","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r5","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r7","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r8","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r1","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r3","BURSEVI:v4p174/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r2","TABATABAI:12:46-49/r10","TABATABAI:12:49/r1","TABATABAI:12:49/r2","TABATABAI:12:49/r5","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r7","TABATABAI:12:56/r3","TABATABAI:12:56/r4","ASAD-NOTES:12:47#n48/r1","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r3","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r4"],"prefer":["TABATABAI:12:46-49/r10","TABATABAI:12:49/r1","TABATABAI:12:56/r3"],"against":["ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r5"]},{"position":"People are relieved and rescued (ghawth) from the distress of famine; God answers their pleas.","reasons":"Derived from quadriliteral aghātha / ghawth, relief (Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn, Bursevi; Biqāʿī: ghawth has only four-letter forms; Ālūsī: God succors them by lifting the hardships pressing on them); rescued from the distress of famine or drought (Wāḥidī, Rāzī, Ṭabrisī, Bayḍāwī, Asad); Baghawī cites the idiom 'I sought help and he helped me'; Ṭabrisī: ghawth is help meeting an urgent need and removing harm; Nasafī: their pleas are answered; Iṣlāḥī prefers it as the literal sense of seeking help, portraying drought's full effects: people cry out and plead with God until answered, and hardship drives even polytheists to call on the one God; Kuranyolu: God helps people; Ṭabāṭabāʾī lists divine relief among the year's blessings.","rows":["THALABI:v5p227/r7","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r6","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r3","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r3","TABRISI:12:43-49#2/r11","TABRISI:12:47-49/r10","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r2","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r4","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r1","BIQAI-FULL:v4p52/r5","BURSEVI:v4p174/r5","BURSEVI:v4p174/r6","ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r3","TABATABAI:12:46-49/r10","TABATABAI:12:49/r5","ASAD-NOTES:12:47#n48/r1","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r3","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r5","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r6","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r7","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:12:49/r1"],"prefer":["ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r5"],"against":["TABATABAI:12:49/r1","TABATABAI:12:56/r3"]},{"position":"People receive abundant produce or vegetation.","reasons":"Ibn ʿĪsā (Māwardī), reported by Ibn al-Jawzī as al-Māwardī's view: relief through abundance; Lisān al-ʿArab (Ṭabāṭabāʾī): ghayth also means vegetation grown from rainwater, which fits the following mention of pressing; Ṭabāṭabāʾī: crops and abundant produce.","rows":["MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r28","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r6","TABATABAI:12:49/r3","TABATABAI:12:49/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Derivation from triliteral ghāth (ghayth, rain) or quadriliteral aghātha (ghawth, relief); fit with the following mention of pressing (Ṭabāṭabāʾī: aid is more natural only under the passive reading yuʿṣarūn); literal versus consequential sense (Iṣlāḥī)."}
{"question":"Why does Joseph speak of 'the people' in the third person here?","words":["ٱلنَّاسُ"],"type":"rhetoric","positions":[{"position":"Joseph shifts from addressing the group directly to saying people will receive rain or aid and press produce, indicating they will no longer need to manage their livelihoods.","reasons":"Ṭabāṭabāʾī.","rows":["TABATABAI:12:49#3/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What does yaʿṣirūn mean?","words":["يَعْصِرُونَ"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"They press produce: grapes, olives, sesame and other fruits, into juice, wine and oil.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAbbās, Mujāhid, Qatāda, al-Ḍaḥḥāk, Ibn Jurayj and most exegetes (Muqātil, Ibn Qutayba, Ṭabarī, Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Thaʿlabī, Māwardī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ibn al-Jawzī via al-ʿAwfī, Abū Ḥayyān, Durr); the produce named varies: grapes, olives, sesame, fruits, oil, sugar cane, radish, dates, syrup; grapes into wine, sesame and olives into oil (Ibn ʿAbbās in Wāḥidī and Durr, Ibn Jurayj, Rāzī); al-Bukhārī records Ibn ʿAbbās's gloss (Qurṭubī); because fruit is plentiful (Māwardī, Bayḍāwī); Ṭabarī prefers it as the unanimous interpretation of the Companions and Successors, which poetry cannot outweigh; Fārisī prefers it, with rescue, since no oil or wine was pressed in famine years; Samīn calls it the clearest sense; Biqāʿī: extracting juices and essences; Ibn Jinnī: Ibn ʿAbbās explains the rescue through pressing; Ibn ʿAṭiyya: al-Naqqāsh records a reading meaning squeezing moisture or similar liquid; Zayd b. ʿAlī's and al-Naqqāsh's readings can also mean squeezing (Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn, Ālūsī); Iṣlāḥī: a large grape harvest; Zajjāj: the yāʾ reading means pressing oil and grapes.","rows":["MUQATIL:12:48-51/r2","ABDURRAZZAQ:v2p324#3/r3","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p218#5/r2","TAB-FULL:v13p195/r1","TAB-FULL:v13p197/r1","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r5","FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r3","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r2","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r3","THALABI:v5p227/r9","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r29","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r7","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:12:47-50/r5","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r4","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r10","TABRISI:12:43-49/r9","TABRISI:12:47-49/r11","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r8","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r5","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r13","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r5","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r13","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v4p393/r2","BIQAI-FULL:v4p52/r6","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r3","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r5","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r7","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r8","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r1","BURSEVI:v4p174/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r4","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r6","TABATABAI:12:49/r4","TABATABAI:12:49/r5","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r3","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r9","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:12:49/r1","STUDYQURAN:12:47/r5"],"prefer":["TAB-FULL:v13p197/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r7"],"against":["TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8"]},{"position":"They are rescued and escape drought and distress, from ʿaṣr / ʿaṣra / ʿuṣra meaning refuge or deliverance.","reasons":"Abū ʿUbayda (Majāz, and as cited by Ibn Qutayba, Fārisī, Thaʿlabī, Māwardī, Wāḥidī, Baghawī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ṭabrisī, Ibn al-Jawzī, Qurṭubī, Abū Ḥayyān, Ālūsī): ʿaṣr, ʿaṣra, ʿuṣra and muʿtaṣar mean refuge or rescue; poetry: the line about the oppressed man, Labīd's muʿṣir, ʿAdī b. Zayd's iʿtiṣār through water, Abū Zubayd's verse calling ʿUthmān a refuge for the distressed, Ibn Muqbil, an anonymous line with iʿtiṣārī, and the line of the thirsty man (Māwardī, Ṭabrisī); Zajjāj (Abū Isḥāq): escaping distress through abundance, and one safe in a stronghold is in ʿaṣr; Fārisī prefers it together with pressing; Zamakhsharī: the passive reading means they are rescued, the active that they rescue themselves; Samīn prefers it because it fits the preceding yughāth, ʿaṣarahu meaning he saved him; the passive readings of Jaʿfar, al-Aʿraj and ʿĪsā mean delivered from hardship, matching yughāth (Ibn Jinnī, Abū Ḥayyān, Ālūsī, Bayḍāwī); Ibn ʿAṭiyya says Ṭabarī rejects it without proof.","rows":["MAJAZ:v1p312/r7","MAJAZ:v1p312/r8","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p218#5/r3","IBNQUTAYBA-GHARIB:v1p218#5/r4","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r4","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r5","FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r3","FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r4","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r2","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r3","THALABI:v5p227/r10","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r32","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p45/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r2","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r7","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r7","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r8","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r9","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r11","TABRISI:12:43-49#3/r1","TABRISI:12:43-49/r10","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r10","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r7","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r8","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r10","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r13","SAMIN-DURR:12:49#2/r1","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r7","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r8","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r10","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r13","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r6"],"prefer":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r7","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r8"],"against":["TAB-FULL:v13p197/r1"]},{"position":"They are rained upon: the clouds are pressed and pour rain, as with the rain clouds (muʿṣirāt) of 78:14, chiefly under the passive reading yuʿṣarūn.","reasons":"The passive reading of ʿĪsā b. ʿUmar, Saʿīd b. Jubayr, Ibn ʿUmar, al-Ṣādiq and ʿAlī means they receive rain, citing 78:14 (Zajjāj, Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Ibn Jinnī, Thaʿlabī, Māwardī, Ibn al-Jawzī, Quṭrub in Samīn, Rāzī, Qurṭubī, Bayḍāwī, Durr, Ṭabāṭabāʾī via al-ʿAyyāshī and al-Qummī); Zamakhsharī: either a verb meaning rain or an omitted preposition (Ibn al-Munīr in Ālūsī likewise; Samīn: ellipsis or metaphor); Ibn al-Mustanīr: rain from a cloud releasing its water, figuratively attributed to them (Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Abū Ḥayyān); al-Jawharī: ʿuṣira al-qawm means a people received rain, so no supplement is needed (Ālūsī); ʿAlī corrected an active recitation, saying they are rained upon, not pressing wine (Ṭabrisī, Ṭabāṭabāʾī); Iṣlāḥī: abundant rain is a consequential sense of the word.","rows":["ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r7","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r2","THALABI:v5p227/r11","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r31","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r6","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r9","TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r13","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r9","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r11","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r11","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r3","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r4","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r3","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8","TABATABAI:12:56/r3","TABATABAI:12:56/r4","TABATABAI:78:13-14/r8","TABATABAI:78:13-14/r9","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r9"],"prefer":["TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8"],"against":[]},{"position":"They milk their animals.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAbbās via ʿAlī b. Abī Ṭalḥa / al-Wālibī (Ṭabarī, Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Thaʿlabī, Wāḥidī, Ibn al-Jawzī, Ibn Kathīr), and Ibn ʿAbbās with Qatāda (Durr); Aḥmad b. ʿUbayd agrees, linking it to abundant livestock and fertile years (Wāḥidī); a cited poem uses the verb for milking (Ibn al-Jawzī); milking is also pressing, squeezing the udder (Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ālūsī); it would indicate abundant vegetation (Qurṭubī); Ṭabāṭabāʾī: milking livestock fits the imagery of the fat and lean cows; a tāʾ reading attributed to Ibn ʿAbbās makes the audience the ones who milk (Ṭabarī, Durr); also listed by Zamakhsharī, Rāzī, Bayḍāwī, Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v13p195/r2","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r6","THALABI:v5p227/r9","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r5","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r9","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r6","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r6","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r6","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r13","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v4p393/r3","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r4","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r5","TABATABAI:12:49#3/r4","TABATABAI:12:49/r4","TABATABAI:12:49/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":["TAB-FULL:v13p197/r2"]},{"position":"They obtain what they desire.","reasons":"Abū ʿUbayd (Wāḥidī) / Abū ʿUbayda (Ibn al-Jawzī), supported by Ibn Aḥmar's verse about taking from life's branches.","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r4","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r11"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They prosper and live well, being given abundant produce.","reasons":"Māwardī: poetic usage yaʿṣiru fīnā for treating us well; some grammarians cited by Ibn al-Anbārī (Ibn al-Jawzī): given abundant produce, making their lives prosperous.","rows":["MAWARDI-FULL:v3p45/r2","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r12"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They give generously and grant gifts.","reasons":"A language scholar cited by Ibn al-Anbārī; al-Azharī reports this sense from Abū ʿUbayd and Abū ʿUbayda (Wāḥidī).","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They bring their livestock from abundant pasture.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAbbās (Māwardī).","rows":["MAWARDI-FULL:v3p42/r30"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"They exploit their produce; iʿtaṣartu mālahu means extracting wealth from someone's hand.","reasons":"Abū al-Ghawth (Qurṭubī), from pressing grapes.","rows":["QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether the root is ʿaṣr 'pressing' (milking being a kind of pressing), ʿaṣra/ʿuṣra 'refuge, rescue', or the rain-bearing clouds of 78:14 under a passive reading; Ṭabarī weighs the Companions' consensus against poetic usage; Samīn weighs fit with the preceding yughāth; Fārisī weighs the contrast with the famine years; ʿAlī objects that pressing wine does not fit."}
{"question":"How is yaʿṣirūn read?","words":["يَعْصِرُونَ"],"type":"readings","positions":[{"position":"Taʿṣirūn, active second person, addressing Joseph's questioners.","reasons":"Ḥamza and al-Kisāʾī (Ibn Mujāhid, Fārisī, Jishumī, Baghawī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ibn al-Jawzī, Abū Ḥayyān and Samīn 'the two brothers', Nasafī for Ḥamza); 'the Kufans except ʿĀṣim' (Thaʿlabī); addresses the petitioners who asked for interpretation (Fārisī, Ibn al-Jawzī; Ṭabrisī: may include others), the king's questioner (Bayḍāwī), the addressees singled out from other people (Ibn Khālawayh), Joseph's interlocutors (Qiraat); in parallel with qaddamtum / continuing the direct address (Jishumī, Baghawī, Thaʿlabī, Samīn); Ṭabarī accepts it as an established reading agreeing in meaning with the yāʾ reading; a tāʾ reading attributed to Ibn ʿAbbās means the audience milk (Ṭabarī, Durr); Zajjāj: taʿṣirūn may mean pressing or escaping distress through abundance; Ālūsī: the special good news suits the king's circumstances, making the second person apt.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v13p195/r2","TAB-FULL:v13p195/r3","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","IBNMUJAHID:v1p349/r2","IBNKHALAWAYH-HUJJA:v1p196/r1","FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r2","THALABI:v5p227/r8","JISHUMI:v5p3652/r5","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r4","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r4","TABRISI:12:43-49/r11","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r7","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r4","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r5","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r4","ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r4","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r2","QIRAAT-ER:12:49:12#2/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Yaʿṣirūn, active third person, referring back to 'the people'.","reasons":"Ibn Kathīr, Nāfiʿ, Abū ʿAmr, ʿĀṣim and Ibn ʿĀmir (Ibn Mujāhid, Fārisī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ibn al-Jawzī; 'the other seven', Abū Ḥayyān); refers back to al-nās in the preceding clause (Ibn Khālawayh, Fārisī, Thaʿlabī, Jishumī, Baghawī, Ṭabrisī; Samīn: matches the absent subject); Ṭabarī: the regional readers transmit it and he accepts both established readings as agreeing in meaning; Zajjāj: it means they press oil and grapes; Qiraat: the standard reading, active Form IV, third-person masculine plural.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v13p195/r3","ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","IBNMUJAHID:v1p349/r2","IBNKHALAWAYH-HUJJA:v1p196/r1","FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r1","THALABI:v5p227/r8","JISHUMI:v5p3652/r5","BAGHAWI-FULL:v4p247#2/r4","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r4","TABRISI:12:43-49/r11","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r5","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r4","QIRAAT-ER:12:49:12#1/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":["TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8"]},{"position":"Yuʿṣarūn, passive third person (ḍamma on yāʾ, fatḥa on ṣād): they are rained upon, or they are rescued.","reasons":"ʿĪsā b. ʿUmar, al-Aʿraj and Jaʿfar b. Muḥammad (Ibn Jinnī, Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Ṭabrisī, Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn, Ālūsī); Saʿīd b. Jubayr (Ibn al-Jawzī); Ibn ʿUmar's recitation (Ibn Abī Ḥātim, via ʿĪsā b. ʿUmar); Ibn al-Mustanīr; al-Ṣādiq and ʿAlī (Ṭabāṭabāʾī, Ṭabrisī), ʿAlī correcting an active recitation because it implies pressing wine during the famine year; meaning rain, as in 78:14 (Zajjāj, Ibn al-Jawzī, Quṭrub, Ibn al-Munīr, al-Jawharī, Bayḍāwī), or rescue, matching yughāth (Ibn Jinnī, Zamakhsharī, Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn, Ālūsī, Rāzī).","rows":["ZAJJAJ:v3p113/r3","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r7","IBNJINNI-MUHTASAB:v1p344/r2","THALABI:v5p227/r11","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r9","TABRISI:12:43-49/r7","TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r13","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r4","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r11","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r9","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r10","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r11","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r3","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8","TABATABAI:12:56/r3","TABATABAI:78:13-14/r8"],"prefer":["TABRISI:12:43-49/r12","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r8"],"against":["TAB-FULL:v13p195/r4"]},{"position":"Tuʿṣarūn, passive second person: you are rained upon, or you are delivered from hardship.","reasons":"ʿĪsā (Qurṭubī, Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn 'ʿĪsā of Basra', Ālūsī); Qurṭubī: it means you are rained upon, as in 78:14, and another reading with kasra on ṣād has the same meaning; Abū Ḥayyān, Ālūsī: you are rescued from hardship, matching yughāth; Durr: a reciter (ʿĪsā b. ʿUbayd al-Thaqafī) reads second-person forms and cites 78:14.","rows":["QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r9","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r10","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r1","DURR-FULL:v4p547/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"A doubled, intensive form yuʿaṣṣirūn, transmitted by al-Naqqāsh.","reasons":"Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn; Ālūsī: with passive yāʾ and kasra on ṣād, conveying abundance or intensity; Ibn ʿAṭiyya: al-Naqqāsh records a reading taking the word as squeezing moisture or similar liquid; Samīn: these readings can mean pressing, milking or rescue.","rows":["IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r10","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r12","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r13","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Taʿaṣṣarūn, from taʿtaṣirūn by assimilating tāʾ into ṣād, read by Zayd b. ʿAlī.","reasons":"Abū Ḥayyān, Samīn (with successive vowel matching), Ālūsī; it can mean squeezing fruit or being rescued.","rows":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r13","SAMIN-DURR:12:49/r12","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether the verb refers back to al-nās or addresses Joseph's questioners; whether it is active (pressing) or passive (rained upon, rescued); Ṭabarī rejects the passive yāʾ reading because it conflicts with the established readings of the regional readers, while ʿAlī rejects the active sense of pressing wine."}
{"question":"Why does the verse mention pressing, and what does it signify?","words":["يَعْصِرُونَ"],"type":"rhetoric","positions":[{"position":"Pressing signals that the famine has ended and prosperity returned, since nothing was pressed during the drought years.","reasons":"Fārisī (also via Wāḥidī): some say they had pressed neither oil nor grapes for fourteen years, no oil or wine being pressed in famine years (Ṭabrisī likewise); Ibn ʿAṭiyya, Abū Ḥayyān: it is reported they pressed nothing in the drought years; Ibn ʿAbbās (Wāḥidī): pressing signals famine's end; Biqāʿī: pressing marks the transition from drought to fertility; Qurṭubī: milking would indicate abundant vegetation; Study Quran: pressing oil and wine signaled food was plentiful again.","rows":["FARISI-HUJJA:v4p425#2/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p139/r7","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v3p251/r6","TABRISI:12:43-49/r9","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r6","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r7","BIQAI-FULL:v4p52/r6","STUDYQURAN:12:47/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The mention suits the king's circumstances: the special good news is apt for him, and mentioning grape pressing subtly suits the king's wine-server.","reasons":"Ālūsī: the good news suits the king's own circumstances, making the second-person reading apt; Iṣlāḥī: grape pressing subtly suits the king's wine-server.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r4","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is mentioned explicitly because rain does not ensure these pressed crops as directly as it ensures grain.","reasons":"Ālūsī.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p255#3/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Rain, fodder growth and milking livestock fit the imagery of the fat and lean cows, explaining the specific mention of rain and pressing.","reasons":"Ṭabāṭabāʾī.","rows":["TABATABAI:12:49#3/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"How did Joseph know of this year: was it part of the dream's interpretation or revealed knowledge of the unseen?","words":["*"],"type":"theology","positions":[{"position":"The year was not in the king's dream nor asked about; God gave Joseph this unseen knowledge by revelation, as a sign of his prophethood.","reasons":"Qatāda (ʿAbd al-Razzāq, Ṭabarī, Ibn Abī Ḥātim, Thaʿlabī, Wāḥidī, Ibn al-Jawzī, Rāzī, Qurṭubī, Abū Ḥayyān, Durr, Ālūsī): God gave Joseph knowledge of an extra, unasked year; Muqātil: it is Joseph's statement, not part of the dream; Ibn ʿAbbās (Durr, Ālūsī): God taught Joseph to volunteer the forecast; Ṭabarī, Ṭabrisī: a sign of prophethood and truthfulness; Wāḥidī: exegetes debate whether the interpretation came from knowledge God taught him or direct revelation, and the unasked year supports direct revelation, which the exegetes say is its only source; Zamakhsharī, Rāzī: the end of famine implies fertility in general, but the detailed promise of relief and pressing needs revelation (Abū Ḥayyān likewise: abundance was expected after seven severe years, revelation specified it); the Manār author (Ṭabāṭabāʾī): the unusually specific forecast is not entailed by the dream; Ālūsī: revelation is apparent, and mere inference would make the prophecy vague, especially in readings describing mutual relief; Abū Ḥayyān: Joseph's reply combines interpretation by meaning, practical advice and a report of the unseen eighth year; Māwardī: possibly prophetic information independent of the dream; Bayḍāwī (and Ṭabāṭabāʾī citing him): revelation is one possibility; Nasafī, Bursevi: by revelation, not from the dream; Kuranyolu: not signaled in the dream.","rows":["MUQATIL:12:48-51/r2","ABDURRAZZAQ:v2p324#3/r3","TAB-FULL:v13p194/r1","TAB-FULL:v13p194/r2","IBNABIHATIM:v7p2154#2/r5","THALABI:v5p227/r5","THALABI:v5p227/r6","MAWARDI-FULL:v3p45/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p136/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v12p138/r2","KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r9","TABRISI:12:43-49#3/r2","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v4p233#3/r4","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#2/r9","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r2","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r5","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p285/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p285/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r15","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r16","BURSEVI:v4p174/r9","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r6","DURR-FULL:v4p546/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r9","TABATABAI:12:49#3/r2","TABATABAI:12:49/r6","TABATABAI:12:49/r7","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:12:49/r1"],"prefer":["KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r9","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r9","TABATABAI:12:49#3/r2","TABATABAI:12:49/r7"],"against":[]},{"position":"Joseph could infer it: the dream's interpretation implied hardship would end after seven years, and God's custom is to send ease after hardship.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭiyya (Abū Ḥayyān): it need not be unseen knowledge, since the interpretation implied abundance after seven years; Bayḍāwī (and Ṭabāṭabāʾī citing him): the expected end of drought or God's custom of relief after hardship; Ṭabāṭabāʾī: the final year is inferred from the repeated seven, after which people need not exert themselves to manage sowing and reserves.","rows":["ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p285/r3","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r5","TABATABAI:12:49/r6","TABATABAI:12:49#2/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":["KASHSHAF-FULL:v2p449/r9","RAZI-FULL:v18p120#3/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r9","TABATABAI:12:49#3/r2","TABATABAI:12:49/r7"]}],"turns_on":"Whether the specific details of the year (rain, relief, pressing) go beyond what the dream implies, or whether the end of hardship after seven years and God's custom of ease after hardship suffice to infer it."}
{"question":"Why does Joseph add, and end with, the good news of this year?","words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"It displays Joseph's merit, knowledge and excellence, as a sign of his prophethood.","reasons":"Ṭabarī, Ṭabrisī: unseen knowledge given as a sign of prophethood and truthfulness; Qurṭubī (with Qatāda): it displays his merit and knowledge; Ālūsī: ending his answer to the king with the glad news, unlike his answer to the prisoners where the prediction came first, highlights his excellence.","rows":["TAB-FULL:v13p194/r1","TABRISI:12:43-49#3/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:12:49/r2","ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It is glad tidings bringing comfort and hope after the discouraging forecast of hardship.","reasons":"Ibn ʿĀshūr: reflects God's pattern of ease after hardship; Bayḍāwī and Bursevi call the final year good news / glad tidings.","rows":["IBNASHUR-FULL:v12p286#3/r8","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r5","BURSEVI:v4p174/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"How do the dream's symbols map onto the years Joseph foretells, and where does this year fall?","words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"Fat cows and green ears signify fertile years, lean cows and dry ears famine years, the swallowing the consumption of stored abundance; this year of relief follows the fourteen years.","reasons":"Rāzī: first seven fertile, next seven drought, as the dream indicates; Bayḍāwī, Bursevi: the swallowing means stored abundance is consumed; Nasafī: the abundant year comes after the fourteen years, a blessed eighth year (Abū Ḥayyān likewise for the symbols); Ibn ʿĀshūr: cows are agricultural years, fatness fertility, leanness drought, ears provisions; al-Qummī (Ṭabāṭabāʾī): seven plentiful years, seven famine years, then rain; Study Quran: the interpretation covers the next fifteen years.","rows":["RAZI-FULL:v18p120#2/r9","BAYDAWI-FULL:12:48-50/r5","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r1","NASAFI-FULL:v2p192#3/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v6p286/r14","BURSEVI:v4p174/r9","IBNASHUR-FULL:v12p286#2/r1","TABATABAI:12:56-57/r5","STUDYQURAN:12:47/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":["TABATABAI:12:49/r8"]},{"position":"The dream is not a forecast of seven fertile then seven barren years; Joseph states the policy required to survive the anticipated famine.","reasons":"Ṭabāṭabāʾī, who says interpreters wrongly treat it as such a forecast.","rows":["TABATABAI:12:49/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether Joseph's words read a sequence of years from the dream's symbols or state a policy for the famine (Ṭabāṭabāʾī)."}
{"question":"Does a dream come to pass according to its first interpretation, given the counselors' dismissal of the king's dream?","words":["*"],"type":"theology","positions":[{"position":"Yes: a dream hangs on a bird's wing until interpreted; dismissing it as confused dreams may not count as an interpretation.","reasons":"Ālūsī, citing the hadith, in reply to al-Balkhī.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r11"],"prefer":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r11"],"against":[]},{"position":"The rule applies only to visions admitting several possible meanings: one interpretation is made and then comes to pass.","reasons":"Ibn al-ʿArabī (Ālūsī).","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r12"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The counselors' dismissal of the king's dream as confused dreams challenges the rule that dreams occur according to their first interpretation.","reasons":"Al-Balkhī (reported and answered by Ālūsī).","rows":[],"prefer":[],"against":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r11"]}],"turns_on":"Whether dismissing a dream as meaningless counts as an interpretation."}
{"question":"What else does the passage teach about dreams and their interpretation?","words":["*"],"type":"theology","positions":[{"position":"An unbeliever can have a true dream; interpreters should not be asked at sunrise, sunset or night; some say a dream seen in sleep is its own interpretation.","reasons":"Reported by Ālūsī, who finds many such claims opaque.","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r13"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Dreams should be interpreted by qualified people.","reasons":"Kuranyolu, since this good year was not signaled in the dream but revealed by God (with reference to 12:4-6).","rows":["KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:12:49/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What do reports say about Joseph's management of the famine?","words":["*"],"type":"reports","positions":[{"position":"Joseph stockpiled the harvests and sold food in the famine for money, then valuables, livestock, servants, property, land and finally people's freedom; then he freed the Egyptians and restored their possessions and kingship.","reasons":"Al-Riḍā (Ṭabāṭabāʾī); Ṭabāṭabāʾī omits many similar reports because most do not directly serve the interpretation of these verses.","rows":["TABATABAI:12:43-57#2/r2","TABATABAI:12:43-57#2/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Joseph tested the coming famine by preparing two portions for each man; when one man ate all of his, Joseph said that was the first day of the hard years.","reasons":"Zayd b. Aslam (Ālūsī).","rows":["ALUSI-FULL:v12p256/r10"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Joseph guided Egypt through the drought, winning the admiration of everyone from the king to ordinary people.","reasons":"Iṣlāḥī.","rows":["ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-4-surah-yunus-10-surah-maryam-19:p174/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What does the Joseph story as a whole illustrate?","words":["*"],"type":"links","positions":[{"position":"Unlike Genesis' romantic account of Joseph's fortunes, the Qur'anic story illustrates God's unfathomable direction of human affairs: people may hate what is good for them or love what is bad for them.","reasons":"Asad, citing 2:216.","rows":["ASAD-NOTES:12:intro/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
{"question":"What is the inward meaning of the year of relief and pressing?","words":["*"],"type":"inward","positions":[{"position":"The fruitful year of relief and pressing signifies the soul's enjoyment after complete tranquility and security.","reasons":"Qāshānī.","rows":["QASHANI:12:49-51/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":""}
